trackpad scroll: content follows from the first delta; host event time; one MOVE per loop turn

cocoa_bridge.mm (macapkScrollAt): the synthesized finger of a two-finger scroll
goes down on the first non-zero delta and starts just past the guest's touch
slop (9pt along the gesture's axis; TOUCH_SLOP is 8dp), instead of being held
back for 6pt and then having to cross the slop like a finger on glass. The guest
enters its drag at once and consumes the slop out of that jump, so every delta
moves the content 1:1, as a macOS scroll view does, and a slow precise scroll is
no longer swallowed. MACAPK_SCROLL_SLOP_JUMP=0 restores the held-back DOWN.

TouchDispatcher.dispatch: a pointer event is stamped with the time the host
delivered it (taken on the AppKit thread), not the time the loop runs it. A loop
busy with a frame runs its queued moves in a burst; stamped then, they read to
VelocityTracker as a finger that stood still and teleported, and the fling after
a scroll came out at a different speed each time. Moves that queue up behind a
slow loop turn are merged into the newest (what InputConsumer's batching does),
so a late frame does not scroll and lay the list out again for positions the
pointer has already left.

Applies on top of 0015 (the Rolagem menu and its settings live there). The
speed setting multiplies the synthesized finger's travel. Inércia "Do macOS":
the finger is kept down for 50ms when the fingers lift and the momentum tail, if
one arrives, continues the same drag; "Do Instagram" (the default) is the old
behaviour, the guest's own fling. Auto-scroll (Rolagem Automática, or
MACAPK_AUTOSCROLL=<secs>) runs a two-finger scroll of 45% of the view in 0.4s
through the same body every N seconds of user idleness, released still moving
at ~600pt/s: released at rest, a 62% drag returned to the same Reel three times
out of three; released moving, the Reels tab advanced one video per step, three
out of three.

